<h2>How the course runs</h2>
<p>Each week works through one or two chapters. Read the module's notes, and ideally the matching chapter, before
  class. The exercises at the end of each module are the best preparation for the assignments and the exams: try each
  derivation yourself before checking it against the notes, and change the code to see what breaks.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Attendance (10%)</strong> — random attendance checks.</li>
  <li><strong>Programming assignments (30%)</strong> — two assignments in PyTorch.</li>
  <li><strong>Midterm exam (30%)</strong> — in week 7, covering modules 01–10.</li>
  <li><strong>Final exam (30%)</strong> — cumulative, with emphasis on modules 11–20.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Getting set up</h2>
<p>The code in the notes needs Python 3 with NumPy, SciPy, PyTorch, and torchvision. A laptop CPU runs every example:
  the models are deliberately small and trained on subsets of MNIST or on synthetic data, and each module takes a few
  seconds to a couple of minutes. A free <a href="https://colab.research.google.com/" rel="noopener">Google Colab</a>
  notebook works too. Paste the cells of a module into a notebook in order and run them. Every random number comes
  from a seeded generator, and the notes were run on one CPU thread, so your output should match closely; on a GPU or
  with several threads, the last digits of training numbers can differ, and timings always will.</p>
